4. Configuration File

Create bd-config.json:

{
  "base_url": "https://blackduck.example.com",
  "api_token": "YOUR_API_TOKEN",
  "token_cache_file": ".bd_token.json"
}

Notes
	•	api_token is the long-lived Black Duck API token
	•	A short-lived bearer token is generated automatically
	•	Bearer token is cached and refreshed when expired

⸻

5. Authentication Flow (Automatic)

Black Duck APIs require a Bearer token, generated from the API token.

Token Generation Endpoint

POST /api/tokens/authenticate

Token Response (example)

{
  "bearerToken": "eyJhbGciOi...",
  "expiresInMilliseconds": 900000
}

The utility:
	1.	Checks for a cached token
	2.	Validates expiration
	3.	Generates a fresh token if needed
	4.	Injects it into every API call

Support engineers never call this manually.

⸻

6. Core Script: bd-cli.sh

Header and Config Load

#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

CONFIG_FILE="bd-config.json"
COLLECTION_FILE="postman/blackduck-sca.json"

BASE_URL=$(jq -r '.base_url' "$CONFIG_FILE")
API_TOKEN=$(jq -r '.api_token' "$CONFIG_FILE")
TOKEN_CACHE=$(jq -r '.token_cache_file' "$CONFIG_FILE")


⸻

7. Bearer Token Management

get_bearer_token() {
  if [[ -f "$TOKEN_CACHE" ]]; then
    EXP=$(jq -r '.expires_at' "$TOKEN_CACHE")
    NOW=$(date +%s)
    if (( NOW < EXP )); then
      jq -r '.token' "$TOKEN_CACHE"
      return
    fi
  fi

  RESPONSE=$(curl -s -X POST \
    "$BASE_URL/api/tokens/authenticate" \
    -H "Authorization: token $API_TOKEN")

  TOKEN=$(echo "$RESPONSE" | jq -r '.bearerToken')
  TTL=$(echo "$RESPONSE" | jq -r '.expiresInMilliseconds')

  EXPIRES_AT=$(( $(date +%s) + TTL / 1000 - 30 ))

  jq -n \
    --arg token "$TOKEN" \
    --argjson exp "$EXPIRES_AT" \
    '{token: $token, expires_at: $exp}' > "$TOKEN_CACHE"

  echo "$TOKEN"
}


