9. Inspecting an API Call (Very Important for Support)

To understand:
	•	Required path variables
	•	Query parameters
	•	Expected request body

Use:

./bd-cli.sh describe "Projects"

Implementation:

describe_command() {
  local NAME="$1"
  jq \
    --arg name "$NAME" \
    '.item[] | select(.name == $name)' \
    "$COLLECTION_FILE"
}

This prints the raw Postman definition, including:
	•	Method (GET/POST/etc)
	•	URL template
	•	Query params
	•	Body schema

This is the source of truth for expected inputs.

⸻

10. Executing an API Call

run_command() {
  local NAME="$1"
  local URL_OVERRIDE="$2"

  TOKEN=$(get_bearer_token)

  METHOD=$(jq -r --arg name "$NAME" '.item[] | select(.name==$name) | .request.method' "$COLLECTION_FILE")
  PATH=$(jq -r --arg name "$NAME" '.item[] | select(.name==$name) | .request.url.raw' "$COLLECTION_FILE")

  URL="$BASE_URL${PATH}"

  curl -s -X "$METHOD" "$URL" \
    -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN" \
    -H "Accept: application/json" | jq
}

Example:

./bd-cli.sh run "Projects"


⸻

11. Handling Filters and Query Parameters

Most Black Duck APIs support filters, limit, offset, sort.

Where to Find Filters
	1.	Run describe
	2.	Inspect:

"query": [
  { "key": "q", "description": "Filter expression" }
]



Example: Passing Filters

./bd-cli.sh run "Projects" "?q=name:my-project&limit=10"

Internally appended to the URL.

⸻

12. Printing Raw vs Pretty Output

Default output is piped to jq.

Raw JSON:

./bd-cli.sh run "Projects" --raw

Filtered output:

./bd-cli.sh run "Projects" | jq '.items[].name'

This encourages jq-first debugging.

